{
  "dom_id": "step-q344949",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/0d6a509ceaa20c53b5ecd031392d5c7ccf8e29bacca857e996602f051ef3bddb.png)\n\nThe graph of the first derivative $y={f}^{′}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-6,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $y=f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-6)$ and $(-1,3):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/4b8010c256447c91b20c0b1c3d2099d7b6739aca5c32ddb4fed357c4a3376759.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is increasing when\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-6\\,)⋃(\\,-1,3\\,).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
